<!-- ============================================================
     WordPress Core wp2shell Pre-Auth RCE
     CVE-2026-63030: REST API batch-route permission desync
     CVE-2026-60137: WP_Query author__not_in SQL injection

     Threat:
       Two-vulnerability chain achieving unauthenticated RCE on
       default WordPress installs with no plugins required.

     CVE-2026-63030 (CVSS 9.8):
       The /wp-json/batch/v1 endpoint processes an array of
       sub-requests. When the first sub-request path is an
       unparseable URL (e.g., http://:), it is added to the
       error list but omitted from the permission-validation
       matches list. Subsequent sub-requests are then evaluated
       against the wrong permission callback, allowing an
       unauthenticated caller to invoke authenticated REST API
       handlers. This permission desync is the root of the
       wp2shell attack name.

     CVE-2026-60137 (chained SQL injection):
       The author__not_in parameter of WP_Query is passed
       unsanitized into the SQL query when accessed via REST API.
       Chained with CVE-2026-63030, an unauthenticated attacker
       extracts all WordPress user password hashes from the
       database via the /wp-json/wp/v2/ REST namespace.

     Full exploit chain (public PoC live July 18, 2026):
       Step 1: POST malformed batch request to /wp-json/batch/v1
               to trigger CVE-2026-63030 permission desync.
       Step 2: Embed author__not_in SQLi payload in a sub-request
               to extract admin password hash (CVE-2026-60137).
       Step 3: Crack hash offline; authenticate as admin.
       Step 4: POST malicious PHP plugin ZIP to
               /wp-admin/update.php?action=upload-plugin.
       Step 5: Activate plugin to achieve code execution as the
               web server user.

     Affected: WordPress 6.9.0-6.9.4, 7.0.0-7.0.1 (full RCE)
               WordPress 6.8.0-6.8.5 (SQLi only, no RCE path)
     Patched:  6.9.5 and 7.0.2
     Public PoC: live July 18, 2026

     MITRE ATT&CK Techniques:
       T1190     - Exploit Public-Facing Application
       T1505.003 - Server Software Component: Web Shell
       T1059.004 - Command and Scripting Interpreter: Unix Shell
       T1078.003 - Valid Accounts: Local Accounts (cracked admin)

     Rule IDs: 104074-104081
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Date: 2026-07-20

     Log source prerequisites:
       Rules 104074-104076, 104078-104081:
         Traefik access log in JSON format forwarded to Wazuh.
         Required fields: RequestMethod, RequestPath, RequestHost,
         DownstreamStatus, ClientHost.
         Decoder: built-in Wazuh JSON decoder (decoded_as=json).
       Rule 104077:
         Full URI including query string must appear in RequestPath.
         Traefik logs the full request URI in RequestPath by default
         in this deployment. If a WAF or separate proxy is used as
         the log source, adjust the field name to match that
         source's full-URI field (e.g., url.full, cs-uri-query).

     Severity tiers:
       0  - POST to /wp-json/batch/v1 (chaining anchor; no alert)
       12 - Any POST to WordPress batch REST endpoint (probe)
       14 - Batch endpoint returned HTTP 2xx (desync may be active)
       14 - author__not_in SQLi pattern in WP REST API (CVE-2026-60137)
       13 - 3 or more batch probes from same source in 60 s (scanner)
       13 - POST to wp-admin plugin upload endpoint (post-exploitation)
       15 - Plugin upload returned 2xx (webshell deployment confirmed)
       15 - PHP file accessed in wp-content upload or mu-plugins path

     References:
       BleepingComputer - WordPress Core wp2shell RCE flaws get
         public exploits, patch now (2026-07-18)
       Rapid7 ETR - CVE-2026-63030 wp2shell Critical RCE in
         WordPress Core (2026-07-18)
       Hadrian - wp2shell: Pre-Auth RCE in WordPress Core REST API
       Aikido Security - Unauthenticated RCE in WordPress (wp2shell)
       NVD CVE-2026-63030:
         https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-63030
       NVD CVE-2026-60137:
         https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-60137
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="initial_access,exploit_pubfacing,wordpress,wp2shell,webshell,cve_2026_63030,cve_2026_60137,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104074: Base anchor - POST to /wp-json/batch/v1
       Traefik access log (JSON decoder)
       Level 0: no alert; chains to rules 104075, 104076, 104078.

       The WordPress REST API batch endpoint at /wp-json/batch/v1
       is rarely exercised in normal WordPress operation. It was
       introduced for Gutenberg editor block requests but no
       first-party WordPress feature sends unauthenticated batch
       REST requests in production. It is the specific
       exploitation target of CVE-2026-63030. Any POST to this
       path warrants investigation and serves as the chaining
       anchor for frequency and success-response rules below.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104074" level="0">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="RequestMethod">POST</field>
    <field name="RequestPath" type="pcre2">(?i)/wp-json/batch/v\d+</field>
    <description>wp2shell CVE-2026-63030: POST to WordPress REST API batch endpoint from $(ClientHost) (base anchor, chains to rules 104075-104076, 104078) (CVE-2026-63030)</description>
    <group>wordpress,wp2shell,initial_access,cve_2026_63030,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104075: CVE-2026-63030 batch-route exploitation probe
       Traefik access log (JSON decoder)
       MITRE: T1190 (Exploit Public-Facing Application)
       Child of rule 104074 (POST to /wp-json/batch/v1).

       The /wp-json/batch/v1 endpoint has no legitimate use case
       in standard WordPress deployments. CVE-2026-63030 is the
       only known mass-exploitation vector targeting this path.
       Level 12 reflects moderate confidence: authenticated
       Gutenberg editor traffic to this path is theoretically
       possible but extremely rare, whereas exploit attempts
       arrive from unauthenticated external IPs.
       Correlate with rule 104077 (author__not_in SQLi) from the
       same source IP to escalate confidence before blocking.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104075" level="12">
    <if_sid>104074</if_sid>
    <description>wp2shell CVE-2026-63030: POST to WordPress /wp-json/batch/v1 from $(ClientHost) to $(RequestHost)$(RequestPath) - batch REST endpoint is rarely used legitimately and is the specific CVE-2026-63030 permission desync target; unauthenticated callers can invoke authenticated REST handlers via sub-request array index misalignment; investigate source IP and correlate with SQLi rule 104077 (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>wordpress,wp2shell,initial_access,exploit_pubfacing,cve_2026_63030,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104076: CVE-2026-63030 batch endpoint returned 2xx
       Traefik access log (JSON decoder)
       MITRE: T1190 (Exploit Public-Facing Application)
       Child of rule 104074 (POST to /wp-json/batch/v1).

       A successful HTTP 2xx response from /wp-json/batch/v1
       indicates WordPress accepted the batch request array.
       In the CVE-2026-63030 exploitation flow, the batch
       request returns HTTP 200 even when the first sub-request
       URL is malformed, because WordPress returns partial
       results for valid sub-requests while silently skipping
       the permission check on subsequent ones. A 2xx response
       is necessary but not sufficient to confirm exploitation;
       escalate when combined with SQLi or plugin upload
       activity from the same source IP within the same window.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104076" level="14">
    <if_sid>104074</if_sid>
    <field name="DownstreamStatus" type="pcre2">^2\d{2}$</field>
    <description>wp2shell CVE-2026-63030: WordPress batch REST API returned $(DownstreamStatus) to $(ClientHost) at $(RequestHost) - successful response from /wp-json/batch/v1 may confirm CVE-2026-63030 permission desync; correlate with author__not_in SQLi (rule 104077) and plugin upload (rule 104079) from same source to confirm RCE chain (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>wordpress,wp2shell,initial_access,exploit_pubfacing,batch_success,cve_2026_63030,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104077: CVE-2026-60137 SQL injection via author__not_in
       Traefik access log (JSON decoder)
       MITRE: T1190 (Exploit Public-Facing Application)

       CVE-2026-60137 is an SQL injection in the WordPress
       WP_Query class author__not_in parameter, accessible via
       the REST API. The parameter is passed unsanitized into the
       SQL query. Exploiters append SQL keywords after the array
       value in the query string, e.g.:
         /wp-json/wp/v2/posts?author__not_in[]=1+UNION+SELECT...
       This regex requires both the author__not_in parameter and
       a SQL injection keyword or encoding pattern in the same
       request URI. Requires Traefik (or proxy) to log the full
       request URI including query string in RequestPath.
       Note: URL-encoded SQL keywords (e.g., %55NION for UNION)
       may bypass this regex; tune with WAF rules for full
       coverage of encoding variants.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104077" level="14">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="RequestPath" type="pcre2">(?i)/wp-json/wp/v\d+/(?:posts|users|pages|media|comments|tags|categories)[^"]*author(?:__|_)not_in[^"]*(?:UNION|SELECT|FROM|SLEEP|BENCHMARK|information_schema|0x[0-9a-fA-F]{4,}|%27|%22|EXTRACTVALUE|UPDATEXML|LOAD_FILE|INTO\+OUTFILE|char\()</field>
    <description>wp2shell CVE-2026-60137: SQL injection via author__not_in parameter in WordPress REST API from $(ClientHost) to $(RequestHost)$(RequestPath) - WP_Query author__not_in is injectable and accessible unauthenticated when chained with CVE-2026-63030 batch desync; attacker extracts admin password hashes from wp_users; correlate with batch endpoint access (rules 104075-104076) from same source (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>wordpress,wp2shell,initial_access,exploit_pubfacing,sqli,cve_2026_60137,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104078: Automated scanner - 3 or more batch/v1 probes
       from same source IP within 60 seconds
       MITRE: T1190 (Exploit Public-Facing Application)

       Automated wp2shell exploit tools send rapid repeated
       POST requests to /wp-json/batch/v1 to probe endpoint
       availability, verify CVE-2026-63030 response behavior,
       and iterate over sub-request payloads for the SQLi
       extraction. Three or more POST requests from the same
       source within 60 seconds is a strong automated-tool
       indicator. Recommend immediate WAF or firewall block
       on the source IP and review of any 2xx responses in
       the preceding window.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104078" level="13" frequency="3" timeframe="60">
    <if_matched_sid>104074</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>ClientHost</same_field>
    <description>wp2shell CVE-2026-63030: 3+ POST requests to WordPress /wp-json/batch/v1 from $(ClientHost) within 60 s - automated exploit tool scanning for CVE-2026-63030 batch permission desync; recommend blocking source IP at WAF; $(RequestHost) is actively targeted (T1190)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>wordpress,wp2shell,initial_access,exploit_pubfacing,scanner,cve_2026_63030,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104079: Post-exploitation - wp-admin plugin upload POST
       Traefik access log (JSON decoder)
       MITRE: T1505.003 (Web Shell), T1078.003 (Valid Accounts)

       After cracking the extracted wp_users password hash, the
       attacker authenticates as an admin and uploads a malicious
       PHP plugin ZIP containing a webshell via the WordPress
       admin plugin upload form. The POST body carries
       action=upload-plugin and the ZIP payload; the Traefik
       access log captures the path and response code. Level 13
       reflects that legitimate admins also install plugins;
       escalate on automated user-agents, unexpected source IPs,
       large request bodies, or when preceded by batch/v1 probe
       activity from the same source within the same session.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104079" level="13">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="RequestMethod">POST</field>
    <field name="RequestPath" type="pcre2">(?i)/wp-admin/(?:update|plugin-install)\.php</field>
    <description>wp2shell post-exploitation: POST to WordPress wp-admin plugin endpoint from $(ClientHost) to $(RequestHost)$(RequestPath) - step 4 of wp2shell chain; after cracking admin hash via CVE-2026-60137 SQLi, attacker uploads malicious PHP plugin ZIP webshell; correlate with preceding batch/v1 and SQLi activity (rules 104075-104077) from same source (T1505.003/T1078.003)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1505</id>
      <id>T1078</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>wordpress,wp2shell,post_exploitation,plugin_upload,webshell,cve_2026_63030,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104080: Plugin upload returned 2xx - webshell deployed
       Traefik access log (JSON decoder)
       MITRE: T1505.003 (Web Shell)
       Child of rule 104079 (wp-admin plugin upload POST).

       A successful HTTP 2xx response from the WordPress plugin
       upload endpoint means the PHP plugin ZIP was accepted
       and extracted into /wp-content/plugins/. The webshell PHP
       file is now web-accessible and ready for command execution.
       Level 15: webshell deployment is confirmed at this point.
       Immediate incident response is required: isolate the host,
       revoke wp-admin credentials, locate and delete the
       malicious plugin directory under wp-content/plugins/.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104080" level="15">
    <if_sid>104079</if_sid>
    <field name="DownstreamStatus" type="pcre2">^2\d{2}$</field>
    <description>wp2shell CRITICAL: WordPress plugin upload returned $(DownstreamStatus) to $(ClientHost) at $(RequestHost) - PHP plugin ZIP accepted and extracted to wp-content/plugins; webshell deployment CONFIRMED via wp2shell chain (CVE-2026-63030/CVE-2026-60137); initiate immediate incident response: isolate host, revoke wp-admin credentials, remove malicious plugin (T1505.003)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1505</id>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>wordpress,wp2shell,post_exploitation,webshell,upload_success,critical,cve_2026_63030,cve_2026_60137,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104081: PHP webshell access in wp-content upload paths
       Traefik access log (JSON decoder)
       MITRE: T1505.003 (Web Shell), T1059.004 (Unix Shell)

       PHP files in /wp-content/uploads/ or /wp-content/mu-plugins/
       should never be directly web-accessible or executable under
       normal WordPress operation. WordPress restricts PHP execution
       in the uploads directory by design (via .htaccess and
       web server config). A PHP file served from those paths
       indicates a webshell placed via a file upload vulnerability.
       This rule also matches PHP files in any wp-content/plugins/
       sub-directory accessed with webshell command parameters
       (cmd=, exec=, system=, etc.), covering the wp2shell plugin
       upload webshell activation step. Access to such a URL means
       the webshell is active and the host is fully compromised.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104081" level="15">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="RequestPath" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:/wp-content/(?:uploads|mu-plugins)/[^"]*\.ph(?:p[3-8]?|tml)(?:\?|$|/)|/wp-content/plugins/[^/]+/[^"]*\.ph(?:p[3-8]?|tml)[^"]*(?:cmd=|command=|exec=|shell=|[?&]c=|run=|system=|pass=|passwd=|whoami=|id=))</field>
    <description>wp2shell: PHP webshell access in WordPress wp-content directory from $(ClientHost) to $(RequestHost)$(RequestPath) - PHP files in wp-content/uploads or mu-plugins should not be web-accessible; PHP files in wp-content/plugins accessed with shell command parameters indicate active webshell execution from wp2shell CVE-2026-63030/CVE-2026-60137 chain; initiate incident response and audit wp-content directory (T1505.003/T1059.004)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1505</id>
      <id>T1059</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>wordpress,wp2shell,webshell,post_exploitation,cve_2026_63030,cve_2026_60137,critical,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
